schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "What Are Tokens? A Beginner's Guide to Digital Assets"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: concept
  summary: >-
    Explains tokens as "digital keys" built on blockchain technology (using
    a theme-park-pass analogy), distinguishes them from native coins,
    contrasts fungible tokens (interchangeable, e.g. cryptocurrencies,
    stablecoins) with non-fungible tokens/NFTs (unique, one-of-a-kind),
    explains how smart contracts execute token logic (vending-machine
    analogy), covers ERC-20/ERC-721/ERC-1155 token standards, and frames
    tokens' relevance to regenerative communities via carbon-credit
    tokenization and cooperative/community-asset ownership.
  audience: permaculture teachers, land stewards, and regenerative communities new to tokens
  known_tensions:
    - tokens-as-speculative-assets vs tokens-as-representations-of-real-value (carbon credits, land stewardship rights)
    - technology-still-evolving vs legitimate volatility/regulation concerns (named directly in the conclusion)
  related_concepts:
    - fungible-vs-nonfungible-tokens
    - token-standards-erc20-erc721-erc1155
  related_resources:
    - Carbonmark
    - Puro.earth
    - KlimaDAO
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-are-tokens.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  source_lineage: what-are-tokens.md
  notes: |
    # assessed-but-excluded
    - Bankless Academy and Ledger Academy are named only as general
      education-platform references in the article's reference list, not
      elaborated on in the body text; excluded as separate resource objects
      to avoid mega-listing thin mentions (small-objects-over-mega-objects,
      but this is the reverse: skip near-empty ones).
    - the M-Pesa mobile-money mention (East Africa) is captured as a
      resource object below rather than excluded, since the article uses
      it as a concrete fungible-token analogy, not just a passing mention;
      no high-risk trigger (it names a public financial system, not
      individuals or exact locations).
